From b79f46b756986b1e64cb6e41af320ca6c3a8323b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kristian Rickert <kristian@apache.org>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:19:48 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] openvino: support Q5_0 weights

The backend takes Q4_0, Q4_1, Q5_1, Q8_0 and the K types as weights but
not Q5_0, so an op over a Q5_0 tensor goes to the CPU backend. Q5_0 is
what most published whisper.cpp quantizations are stored as, and
whisper.cpp aborts when it builds a state over weights the selected
backend does not take.

A Q5_0 block is an fp16 scale d, 32 high bits and 32 low nibbles, and
a weight is d * (q - 16) with q in [0, 31]. It is symmetric, so it maps
onto the layout Q8_0 already uses: signed i8 weights (q - 16), one f16
scale per group of 32, no zero point. extract_q5_0_data unpacks the
5-bit values; the rest is adding the type next to Q8_0 and Q5_1 in the
layout, extraction, weight-type and supported-type lists. 3D expert
weights follow Q8_0 too, including the 4-bit requantization on GPU.

Checked against ggml's CPU dequantizer with a tensor that has every q
at every position: GET_ROWS returns the same values bit for bit on the
CPU device and on an Arc B70; MUL_MAT returns them at the device's
inference precision (largest relative difference 3.9e-4 on the B70 in
f16, 3.5e-3 on a CPU that runs bf16). A Whisper model quantized to q5_0
runs with all four graphs on the device and transcribes the same words
as the CPU backend, on both devices.

test-backend-ops was not run; 3D Q5_0 expert weights are untested.
